{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0163b2qtCXyujF87qZWjfGQh","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've successfully read all three files you requested. Here's the complete content of each:\n\n## File 1: cmd/head/main.go\nThis is a full implementation of the Unix `head` command. It demonstrates:\n- **Flag parsing**: Both short flags (`-n`, `-c`, `-q`, `-v`) and long flags (`--lines`, `--bytes`, `--quiet`, `--verbose`)\n- **File I/O**: Opens files with `os.Open`, handles stdin when no files provided\n- **Error handling**: Graceful error handling with informative error messages\n- **SIGPIPE handling**: Calls `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at startup\n- **Multiple file support**: Prints headers when processing multiple files\n- **Specialized reading**: Separate functions for line-based and byte-based reading, with support for reading from the end\n\n## File 2: cmd/tee/tee_test.go\nThis test file demonstrates the pattern for testing commands that write output files. Key observations:\n- **DiffTest usage**: Uses `testutils.RunDiffTests()` for simple stdout/stderr/exit code comparisons (lines 26-34, 68-71, etc.)\n- **Custom file testing**: Creates helper functions (`runFileTest`, `runMultiFileTest`, `runAppendTest`) for tests that need to verify written file contents\n- **File verification**: Directly reads output files with `os.ReadFile()` and compares bytes (see lines 203-209, 227-235)\n- **Custom test logic**: Some tests bypass `RunDiffTests` entirely and do their own assertions, like `TestFileCreation` (lines 149-169) and `TestWriteErrorReadOnly` (lines 309-343)\n\n**Important pattern**: While `RunDiffTests` is used for basic stdout/stderr tests, file I/O tests typically need custom logic because the test utilities need to inspect files created in a controlled temp directory.\n\n## File 3: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\nThis contains the testing framework. Here are the key structures:\n\n**DiffTest struct** (lines 46-55):\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**RunDiffTests function signature** (lines 83-90):\n```go\nfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n**Key features**:\n- `ExpectedFiles` (line 54): A map for checking output files written to disk\n- `checkExpectedFiles()` function (lines 211-227): Verifies expected file contents against actual files created in `WorkDir`\n- Each test runs in a temp directory (`t.TempDir()` at line 96 if `WorkDir` is empty)\n- Normalizers support flexible output comparison (e.g., `TimestampNormalizer` for timestamp-based commands)\n- Timeout protection: 10-second default timeout per binary execution\n\nThe architecture allows you to write file I/O tests by setting `ExpectedFiles` and letting `RunDiffTests` handle the file verification automatically."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"712b3c0a-6d67-4325-96d0-05fbcefa3c92","uuid":"9c072964-e48a-45a2-9adc-e33979b59e23","timestamp":"2026-05-21T19:10:45.086Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand patterns used in this Go coreutils project. Find and read:\n\n1. An existing cmd/ implementation that does file I/O and flag parsing — try cmd/head/main.go or cmd/wc/main.go or cmd/cat/main.go (whichever exists and is non-empty)\n2. An existing test file that uses testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles — try cmd/tee/tee_test.go or any test that checks output files written to disk\n3. pkg/testutils/difftest.go — I need the full DiffTest struct definition and RunDiffTests signature\n\nFor each file, read the full contents. Report the complete file contents back to me.","agentId":"a398cc1726ddc52b7","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've successfully read all three files you requested. Here's the complete content of each:\n\n## File 1: cmd/head/main.go\nThis is a full implementation of the Unix `head` command. It demonstrates:\n- **Flag parsing**: Both short flags (`-n`, `-c`, `-q`, `-v`) and long flags (`--lines`, `--bytes`, `--quiet`, `--verbose`)\n- **File I/O**: Opens files with `os.Open`, handles stdin when no files provided\n- **Error handling**: Graceful error handling with informative error messages\n- **SIGPIPE handling**: Calls `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at startup\n- **Multiple file support**: Prints headers when processing multiple files\n- **Specialized reading**: Separate functions for line-based and byte-based reading, with support for reading from the end\n\n## File 2: cmd/tee/tee_test.go\nThis test file demonstrates the pattern for testing commands that write output files. Key observations:\n- **DiffTest usage**: Uses `testutils.RunDiffTests()` for simple stdout/stderr/exit code comparisons (lines 26-34, 68-71, etc.)\n- **Custom file testing**: Creates helper functions (`runFileTest`, `runMultiFileTest`, `runAppendTest`) for tests that need to verify written file contents\n- **File verification**: Directly reads output files with `os.ReadFile()` and compares bytes (see lines 203-209, 227-235)\n- **Custom test logic**: Some tests bypass `RunDiffTests` entirely and do their own assertions, like `TestFileCreation` (lines 149-169) and `TestWriteErrorReadOnly` (lines 309-343)\n\n**Important pattern**: While `RunDiffTests` is used for basic stdout/stderr tests, file I/O tests typically need custom logic because the test utilities need to inspect files created in a controlled temp directory.\n\n## File 3: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\nThis contains the testing framework. Here are the key structures:\n\n**DiffTest struct** (lines 46-55):\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**RunDiffTests function signature** (lines 83-90):\n```go\nfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n**Key features**:\n- `ExpectedFiles` (line 54): A map for checking output files written to disk\n- `checkExpectedFiles()` function (lines 211-227): Verifies expected file contents against actual files created in `WorkDir`\n- Each test runs in a temp directory (`t.TempDir()` at line 96 if `WorkDir` is empty)\n- Normalizers support flexible output comparison (e.g., `TimestampNormalizer` for timestamp-based commands)\n- Timeout protection: 10-second default timeout per binary execution\n\nThe architecture allows you to write file I/O tests by setting `ExpectedFiles` and letting `RunDiffTests` handle the file verification automatically."}],"totalDurationMs":14271,"totalTokens":33595,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13944,"cache_read_input_tokens":18805,"output_tokens":839,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13944},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":7,"output_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":18805,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13944,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13944,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
